<section xml:id="sec-mod5-study-guide">
  <title>Companion Reading and Study Guide</title>
  <p>Reading: Zorn, Sections 2.5<ndash/>2.6. Estimated time: 55 minutes.</p>

    <p>
      This guide accompanies two sections of Zorn's <em>Understanding
      Real Analysis</em>. Section 2.5 (<q>Series 101: Basic Ideas</q>)
      introduces the formal definition of series convergence via partial
      sums, the geometric series, and the basic algebra of convergent
      series. Section 2.6 (<q>Series 102: Testing for Convergence</q>)
      develops the named tests: Comparison, Limit Comparison, Ratio,
      Root, and Alternating Series, along with a brief treatment of
      absolute convergence.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read each section before working its questions. Because many of
      the tests may be familiar from a calculus course, pay particular
      attention to the <em>statements</em> and <em>proofs</em>&#x2014;not
      just the recipes. The recurring theme, which you should be able
      to articulate by the end of this guide, is that every test is
      ultimately a statement about the partial-sum sequence <m>(S_n)</m>
      or (for absolute convergence) <m>(T_n) = \sum_{k=1}^{n} |a_k|</m>.
    </p>

  <!-- ============================================================
       SECTION 2.5: DEFINITION AND BASIC IDEAS
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod5-sg-25-intro">
    <title>Section 2.5: Series 101 — Basic Ideas (Zorn pp. 119&#x2013;134)</title>
    <p>
      Read Zorn's Definition 2.22 (series convergence) very carefully. This
      is the definition on which all of Module 5 rests. Notice that it
      is not a new kind of limit&#x2014;it is the old <xref ref="ch-mod4"/> limit,
      applied to the partial-sum sequence.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-sg-25-1">
    <title>Parsing the Definition</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write Zorn's Definition 2.22 (convergence of a series)
        symbolically. Your statement should involve the partial sums
        <m>S_n = a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_n</m> and the
        <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> definition of sequence convergence
        from <xref ref="ch-mod4"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            What is the type of the object <m>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} a_k</m>?
            (Is it a number? A sequence? A symbol for a limit?)
          </li>
          <li>
            If asked to prove <q><m>\sum a_k</m> converges to <m>S</m></q>,
            which specific sequence must you show converges to <m>S</m>,
            and by what definition?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) When the series converges, <m>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} a_k</m>
        denotes the real number <m>\lim_{n \to \infty} S_n</m>. When
        it diverges, the symbol has no numerical value (though we
        sometimes write <m>\sum a_k = \infty</m> loosely for
        divergence to infinity).
        (b) You must show that the partial-sum sequence <m>(S_n)</m>
        converges to <m>S</m> in the <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> sense:
        <m>\forall \varepsilon \gt 0\ \exists N \in \N\ \forall n \geq N:
        |S_n - S| \lt \varepsilon</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-sg-25-2">
    <title>The Geometric Series Computation</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Zorn derives the formula
        <md>\sum_{k=1}^{n} ar^{k-1} = a \cdot \frac{1 - r^n}{1 - r}
        \quad (r \neq 1),</md>
        and then takes <m>n \to \infty</m> to evaluate the infinite
        sum when <m>|r| \lt 1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Prove the closed-form partial-sum identity by induction
            on <m>n</m>. (The base case is <m>n = 1</m>.)
          </li>
          <li>
            Explain why <m>r^n \to 0</m> when <m>|r| \lt 1</m>. (You
            do not need to prove this rigorously, but you should be
            able to point at the <xref ref="ch-mod4"/> tool that does the work;
            in Zorn it appears as Theorem 2.16 or near Proposition 2.24.)
          </li>
          <li>
            Conclude: when <m>|r| \lt 1</m>,
            <m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} ar^{k-1} = \frac{a}{1 - r}</m>.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) Base: <m>n = 1</m> gives <m>a = a(1 - r)/(1 - r)</m>.
        Induction step: if the formula holds for <m>n</m>, add
        <m>ar^n</m> to both sides and simplify to get the formula
        with <m>n + 1</m> in place of <m>n</m>.
        (b) For <m>|r| \lt 1</m>, the sequence <m>(|r|^n)</m> is
        decreasing and bounded below by 0; MCT gives a limit <m>L \geq 0</m>.
        Since <m>|r|^{n+1} = |r| \cdot |r|^n</m>, passing to the limit
        on both sides gives <m>L = |r| \cdot L</m>, so
        <m>L (1 - |r|) = 0</m>, so <m>L = 0</m>. Since
        <m>|r^n - 0| = |r|^n</m>, this gives <m>r^n \to 0</m>.
        (c) Using the sum and quotient rules from the Algebra of Limits
        together with <m>r^n \to 0</m> gives
        <m>a(1 - r^n)/(1 - r) \to a \cdot 1/(1 - r)</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-sg-25-3">
    <title>The Divergence Test as a Corollary of Module 4</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Read Zorn's statement of the Divergence Test (the
        <q>nth-term test</q>): if <m>\sum a_k</m> converges, then
        <m>a_k \to 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Prove this from scratch, using the definition of series
        convergence and a <xref ref="ch-mod4"/> limit law of your choice.
      </p>
      <p>
        (Hint: <m>a_n = S_n - S_{n-1}</m>, where <m>S_0 = 0</m>.)
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        Assume <m>\sum a_k</m> converges, so <m>S_n \to S</m> for
        some <m>S \in \R</m>. Set <m>S_0 = 0</m>, so that
        <m>a_n = S_n - S_{n-1}</m> for every <m>n \geq 1</m>. The
        sequence <m>(S_{n-1})_{n \geq 1} = (S_0, S_1, S_2, \ldots)</m> is
        obtained by shifting <m>(S_n)</m> back by one index, and it has
        the same limit: given <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, choose <m>N</m>
        with <m>|S_n - S| \lt \varepsilon</m> for all <m>n \geq N</m>;
        then <m>|S_{n-1} - S| \lt \varepsilon</m> for all
        <m>n \geq N + 1</m>. So <m>S_{n-1} \to S</m> as well. By the
        difference rule (Algebra of Limits),
        <m>a_n = S_n - S_{n-1} \to S - S = 0</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-sg-25-4">
    <title>The Harmonic Series as Counterexample</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <em>converse</em> of the Divergence Test is false: there
        exist series with <m>a_k \to 0</m> that still diverge. Zorn
        emphasizes the harmonic series <m>\sum 1/k</m> as the
        standard example.
      </p>
      <p>
        Write <m>H_n = 1 + \frac{1}{2} + \cdots + \frac{1}{n}</m> for
        the <m>n</m>th partial sum of the harmonic series.
        Reproduce one of the proofs that <m>\sum 1/k</m> diverges.
        The cleanest is the classical grouping argument (sometimes
        attributed to Oresme in the 14th century):
        <md>H_{2^m} = 1 + \frac{1}{2}
        + \left(\frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{4}\right)
        + \left(\frac{1}{5} + \cdots + \frac{1}{8}\right)
        + \cdots</md>
        Show that each parenthesized block is bounded below by
        <m>1/2</m>, and conclude that <m>H_{2^m} \geq 1 + m/2</m>, so
        <m>H_{2^m} \to \infty</m>, hence <m>(H_n)</m> diverges.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        In the block <m>\frac{1}{2^k + 1} + \frac{1}{2^k + 2} + \cdots
        + \frac{1}{2^{k+1}}</m>, each of the <m>2^k</m> summands is
        at least <m>1/2^{k+1}</m>, so the block is at least
        <m>2^k \cdot 1/2^{k+1} = 1/2</m>. Summing <m>m</m> such blocks,
        <m>H_{2^m} \geq 1 + m/2</m>. Since <m>m/2 \to \infty</m>, so
        does <m>H_{2^m}</m>, and the harmonic series diverges.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-sg-25-5">
    <title>Algebra with Convergent Series</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Read Zorn's Theorem 2.23 (algebra with convergent series).
        State the results for sums and constant multiples, and explain
        in one sentence why each is a direct consequence of the
        Algebra of Limits from <xref ref="ch-mod4"/> applied to partial sums.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        If <m>\sum a_n = A</m> and <m>\sum b_n = B</m>, then
        <m>\sum (a_n \pm b_n) = A \pm B</m> and <m>\sum c a_n = c A</m>
        for any constant <m>c</m>. Each follows by applying the
        sum rule (resp. constant-multiple rule) from <xref ref="ch-mod4"/> to the
        partial-sum sequences <m>(S_n^{(a)})</m> and <m>(S_n^{(b)})</m>:
        the partial sum of <m>a_n + b_n</m> is <m>S_n^{(a)} + S_n^{(b)}</m>,
        and limits of sums are sums of limits.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       SECTION 2.6: TESTS
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod5-sg-26-intro">
    <title>Section 2.6: Series 102 — Testing for Convergence (Zorn pp. 134&#x2013;146)</title>
    <p>
      Section 2.6 is a catalog of convergence tests. Read slowly,
      because this is where the recipes get proved. For each test,
      identify: the hypothesis on the terms, the conclusion about the
      series, and the earlier fact that drives the proof: a result
      from <xref ref="ch-mod4"/> (MCT,
      the Cauchy criterion, or the fact that
      <xref ref="par-mod4-sg-two-facts" text="custom">limits preserve
      non-strict inequalities</xref>) or the geometric series of
      <xref ref="ex-mod5-sg-25-2"/>.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-sg-26-1">
    <title>The Comparison Test</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        State Zorn's Comparison Test (Theorem 2.26 or its analogue):
        if <m>0 \leq a_k \leq b_k</m> for all <m>k</m>, then
        (i) <m>\sum b_k</m> converges <m>\Rightarrow</m> <m>\sum a_k</m>
        converges, and (ii) <m>\sum a_k</m> diverges <m>\Rightarrow</m>
        <m>\sum b_k</m> diverges.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then answer:
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Why is (i) the contrapositive of (ii)?
          </li>
          <li>
            In the proof of (i), where is the Monotone Convergence
            Theorem used, and what object does it produce?
          </li>
          <li>
            Why is the hypothesis <m>a_k, b_k \geq 0</m> essential?
            What could go wrong if the terms could be negative?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) Direct restatement: <m>P \Rightarrow Q</m> and
        <m>\neg Q \Rightarrow \neg P</m> are logically equivalent.
        (b) The partial sums <m>S_n^{(a)}</m> and <m>S_n^{(b)}</m>
        are both increasing (because terms are nonnegative). If
        <m>\sum b_k = B</m>, then <m>S_n^{(b)} \leq B</m> for all
        <m>n</m>, so <m>S_n^{(a)} \leq S_n^{(b)} \leq B</m>, so
        <m>(S_n^{(a)})</m> is increasing and bounded above. MCT
        delivers a limit.
        (c) Without nonnegativity, <m>(S_n^{(a)})</m> need not be
        monotone, so MCT does not apply; moreover the inequality
        <m>a_k \leq b_k</m> by itself does not prevent partial sums
        from oscillating wildly. Even (i) fails: <m>a_k = -1</m> and
        <m>b_k = 0</m> satisfy <m>a_k \leq b_k</m>, and <m>\sum b_k</m>
        converges (to 0), but <m>S_n^{(a)} = -n</m>, so
        <m>\sum a_k</m> diverges.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-sg-26-2">
    <title>The Limit Comparison Test</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        State Zorn's Limit Comparison Test (Theorem 2.29): for series
        <m>\sum a_k</m> and <m>\sum b_k</m> with positive terms, if
        <m>\lim_{k \to \infty} a_k / b_k = L \in (0, \infty)</m>, then
        <m>\sum a_k</m> and <m>\sum b_k</m> either both converge or
        both diverge.
      </p>
      <p>
        Apply the test to determine whether
        <m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{k^2 + k}</m>
        converges, by comparison with <m>\sum 1/k^2</m>. (You may
        take <m>\sum 1/k^2</m> as a known convergent series; Zorn
        proves this both directly and via the <m>p</m>-series
        theorem.)
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        Let <m>a_k = 1/(k^2 + k)</m> and <m>b_k = 1/k^2</m>. Then
        <m>a_k/b_k = k^2/(k^2 + k) = 1/(1 + 1/k) \to 1 \in (0, \infty)</m>
        by the algebra of limits. Since <m>\sum 1/k^2</m> converges,
        Limit Comparison implies <m>\sum 1/(k^2 + k)</m> converges.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-sg-26-3">
    <title>The Ratio Test</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        State Zorn's Ratio Test: for <m>\sum a_k</m> with positive
        terms, if <m>\lim_{k \to \infty} a_{k+1}/a_k = L</m>, then the
        series converges if <m>L \lt 1</m>, diverges if <m>L \gt 1</m>,
        and the test is inconclusive at <m>L = 1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Sketch the proof of the <m>L \lt 1</m> case. The idea is
            to pick any <m>r</m> with <m>L \lt r \lt 1</m>, use the
            hypothesis to produce an index <m>N</m> past which
            <m>a_{k+1}/a_k \lt r</m>, and then bound
            <m>a_{N+j} \leq a_N \cdot r^j</m>. Invoke the geometric
            series.
          </li>
          <li>
            Why does the test fail at <m>L = 1</m>? Give one series
            with <m>L = 1</m> that converges and one that diverges.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) Given <m>L \lt 1</m>, choose <m>r = (L + 1)/2 \in (L, 1)</m>.
        Pick <m>N</m> with <m>a_{k+1}/a_k \lt r</m> for <m>k \geq N</m>.
        Induction gives <m>a_{N+j} \leq a_N r^j</m> for all
        <m>j \geq 0</m>. Let <m>C = a_1 + \cdots + a_{N-1}</m>
        (<m>C = 0</m> if <m>N = 1</m>). The partial sums <m>S_n</m>
        increase because <m>a_k \gt 0</m>, and for <m>n \geq N</m> the
        partial-sum formula of <xref ref="ex-mod5-sg-25-2"/> gives
        <m>S_n \leq C + a_N \sum_{j=0}^{n-N} r^j
        = C + a_N (1 - r^{n-N+1})/(1 - r) \leq C + a_N/(1 - r)</m>.
        So <m>(S_n)</m> is increasing and bounded above by a finite
        constant, and the Monotone Convergence Theorem gives
        convergence of <m>\sum a_k</m>.
        (b) Harmonic series <m>\sum 1/k</m>: ratio
        <m>k/(k+1) \to 1</m>; diverges.
        <m>p</m>-series <m>\sum 1/k^2</m>: ratio
        <m>k^2/(k+1)^2 \to 1</m>; converges.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod5-sg-26-root">
    <title>The Root Test</title>
    <p>
      The Ratio Test compares each term with the one before it. The
      Root Test compares each term <m>a_k</m> directly with a power
      <m>r^k</m> of a fixed number. This module uses it in the two
      forms below; the limit form is the version you may know from
      calculus. The proofs compare with the geometric series and use
      only results from <xref ref="ch-mod4"/>
      and from this module. Every nonnegative real number has a unique
      nonnegative <m>k</m>th root (a consequence of the completeness of
      <m>\R</m>; it also follows from the Intermediate Value Theorem in
      <xref ref="ch-mod6"/>), and the usual
      exponent laws apply to these roots. For <m>a_k \geq 0</m>,
      <m>\sqrt[k]{a_k}</m> denotes the nonnegative <m>k</m>th root of
      <m>a_k</m>.
    </p>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-mod5-root-test-bound">
      <title>Root Test, Eventual-Bound Form</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>a_k \geq 0</m> for all <m>k</m>, and there exist a
          number <m>r</m> with <m>0 \leq r \lt 1</m> and an index <m>N</m>
          such that <m>\sqrt[k]{a_k} \leq r</m> for all <m>k \geq N</m>.
          Then <m>\sum a_k</m> converges.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Raising both sides of
          <m>\sqrt[k]{a_k} \leq r</m> to the <m>k</m>th power (both sides
          are nonnegative) gives <m>a_k \leq r^k</m> for all
          <m>k \geq N</m>. Let <m>C = a_1 + \cdots + a_{N-1}</m>, with
          <m>C = 0</m> if <m>N = 1</m>. The partial sums <m>S_n</m> are
          increasing because <m>a_k \geq 0</m>, and <m>S_n \leq C</m> for
          <m>n \lt N</m>. For <m>n \geq N</m>, the partial-sum formula of
          <xref ref="ex-mod5-sg-25-2"/> (with first term <m>r^N</m> and
          ratio <m>r</m>) gives
          <md>
            <mrow>S_n \amp\leq C + \sum_{k=N}^{n} r^k
            = C + r^N \cdot \frac{1 - r^{n-N+1}}{1 - r}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp\leq C + \frac{r^N}{1 - r}.</mrow>
          </md>
          So <m>(S_n)</m> is increasing and bounded above, and the
          Monotone Convergence Theorem says it converges.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-mod5-root-test-limit">
      <title>Root Test, Limit Form</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>a_k \geq 0</m> for all <m>k</m> and
          <m>\sqrt[k]{a_k} \to \rho</m>. Then <m>\sum a_k</m> converges if
          <m>\rho \lt 1</m> and diverges if <m>\rho \gt 1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Every root <m>\sqrt[k]{a_k}</m> is at least 0, so
          <m>\rho \geq 0</m>, because limits preserve non-strict
          inequalities (<xref ref="thm-mod4-limit-order"/>).
          Suppose <m>\rho \lt 1</m>, and set <m>r = (\rho + 1)/2</m>, so
          that <m>0 \leq \rho \lt r \lt 1</m>. The definition of
          convergence with <m>\varepsilon = r - \rho</m> gives an
          <m>N</m> with <m>\sqrt[k]{a_k} \lt \rho + \varepsilon = r</m> for
          all <m>k \geq N</m>, and the eventual-bound form shows that
          <m>\sum a_k</m> converges. Now suppose <m>\rho \gt 1</m>. The
          definition of convergence with <m>\varepsilon = \rho - 1</m>
          gives an <m>N</m> with
          <m>\sqrt[k]{a_k} \gt \rho - \varepsilon = 1</m>, and hence
          <m>a_k \gt 1</m>, for all <m>k \geq N</m>. Then
          <m>a_k \not\to 0</m> (if <m>a_k \to 0</m>, then
          <m>a_k \lt 1</m> for all large <m>k</m>), so <m>\sum a_k</m>
          diverges by the Divergence Test.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
    <p>
      If <m>\rho = 1</m>, the test is inconclusive. To see this,
      compare
      <m>\sum 1/k</m>, which diverges
      (<xref ref="ex-mod5-sg-25-4"/>), with <m>\sum 1/k^2</m>, which
      converges (the <m>p</m>-series theorem with <m>p = 2</m>; see
      <xref ref="ex-mod5-sg-26-6"/>). For both, <m>\sqrt[k]{a_k} \to 1</m>.
      Once you know that <m>k^{1/k} \to 1</m>, the quotient and product
      rules give <m>\sqrt[k]{1/k} = 1/k^{1/k} \to 1</m> and
      <m>\sqrt[k]{1/k^2} = (1/k^{1/k})^2 \to 1</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The limit <m>k^{1/k} \to 1</m> does not follow from the Algebra
      of Limits alone, because the base and the exponent both change
      with <m>k</m>. We prove it with <term>Bernoulli's inequality</term>
      (<xref ref="thm-mod5-bernoulli"/>).
    </p>
    <lemma xml:id="thm-mod5-bernoulli">
      <title>Bernoulli's Inequality</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For every <m>h \geq 0</m> and every integer <m>k \geq 1</m>,
          <m>(1 + h)^k \geq 1 + kh</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          The proof is by induction on <m>k</m>. For
          <m>k = 1</m>, both sides equal <m>1 + h</m>. If the inequality
          holds for <m>k</m>, multiplying both sides by
          <m>1 + h \geq 1 \gt 0</m> preserves it, so
          <m>(1 + h)^{k+1} \geq (1 + kh)(1 + h) = 1 + (k+1)h + kh^2
          \geq 1 + (k+1)h</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </lemma>
    <p>
      Now let <m>h_k = k^{1/(2k)} - 1</m>, which
      is at least 0 because <m>k \geq 1</m>. Bernoulli's inequality
      gives <m>\sqrt{k} = (1 + h_k)^k \geq 1 + k h_k</m>, so
      <md>0 \leq h_k \leq \frac{\sqrt{k} - 1}{k} \lt \frac{1}{\sqrt{k}}.</md>
      Hence <m>1 \leq k^{1/k} = (1 + h_k)^2 \lt (1 + 1/\sqrt{k})^2</m>.
      Also <m>1/\sqrt{k} \to 0</m>: given <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>,
      the Archimedean Property gives an <m>N</m> with
      <m>N \gt 1/\varepsilon^2</m>, and then every <m>k \geq N</m>
      satisfies <m>1/\sqrt{k} \leq 1/\sqrt{N} \lt \varepsilon</m>.
      So the right-hand side tends to 1 by the Algebra of Limits, and
      the Squeeze Theorem gives <m>k^{1/k} \to 1</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The eventual-bound form asks for less than the limit form. It
      applies even when <m>\lim \sqrt[k]{a_k}</m> does not exist, as
      long as the roots eventually stay at or below a fixed
      <m>r \lt 1</m>; you will meet such a series in
      <xref ref="ex-mod5-ps-c2"/>. The bound must be a fixed number
      less than 1; a bound of 1 is not enough: for <m>a_k = 1/k</m>,
      every root <m>\sqrt[k]{1/k}</m> with <m>k \geq 2</m> is less than
      1, yet <m>\sum 1/k</m> diverges.
    </p>
    <p>
      Many texts state the Root Test with
      <m>\limsup \sqrt[k]{a_k}</m> in place of
      <m>\lim \sqrt[k]{a_k}</m>. When you meet that version, whether
      in the MAT 5610 text or elsewhere, recognize it as a sharper form
      of the eventual-bound form (it also decides divergence), whose
      proof of convergence is exactly the argument above. You will
      compare statements in <xref ref="ex-mod5-br-3"/>.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-sg-26-4">
    <title>Absolute Convergence</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Read Zorn's discussion of absolute convergence. A series
        <m>\sum a_k</m> converges <em>absolutely</em> if the series
        <m>\sum |a_k|</m> of absolute values converges.
      </p>
      <p>
        The central theorem is: <em>absolute convergence implies
        convergence.</em> Outline the proof in two or three sentences.
        (Hint: use the Cauchy criterion for the partial sums
        <m>(S_n)</m>, together with the triangle inequality applied
        to finite sums.)
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        Let <m>T_n = \sum_{k=1}^{n} |a_k|</m>. If <m>(T_n)</m>
        converges, it is Cauchy: for every <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>
        there exists <m>N</m> with <m>T_n - T_m = \sum_{k=m+1}^{n} |a_k|
        \lt \varepsilon</m> for <m>n \gt m \geq N</m>. By the triangle
        inequality, <m>|S_n - S_m| = \left|\sum_{k=m+1}^{n} a_k\right|
        \leq \sum_{k=m+1}^{n} |a_k| \lt \varepsilon</m>, so <m>(S_n)</m>
        is Cauchy, and by Cauchy completeness of <m>\R</m> (<xref ref="ch-mod4"/>),
        <m>(S_n)</m> converges.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-sg-26-5">
    <title>The Alternating Series Test</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        State the Alternating Series Test: if <m>(b_k)</m> is a
        positive, decreasing sequence with <m>b_k \to 0</m>, then
        <m>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} (-1)^{k+1} b_k</m> converges.
      </p>
      <p>
        Read Zorn's proof and answer:
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            What is the sequence of <em>even</em> partial sums,
            <m>(S_{2m})</m>? Is it monotone, and bounded? Conclusion?
          </li>
          <li>
            Same question for the <em>odd</em> partial sums
            <m>(S_{2m+1})</m>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Why do the two subsequences have the <em>same</em> limit,
            and why does that imply the full sequence <m>(S_n)</m>
            converges?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>S_{2m} = (b_1 - b_2) + (b_3 - b_4) + \cdots + (b_{2m-1} - b_{2m})</m>;
        each group is nonnegative, so <m>(S_{2m})</m> is increasing.
        Also <m>S_{2m} = b_1 - (b_2 - b_3) - \cdots - (b_{2m-2} - b_{2m-1}) - b_{2m} \leq b_1</m>,
        so bounded above. MCT gives a limit <m>L</m>.
        (b) <m>S_{2m+1} = S_{2m} + b_{2m+1}</m>; since
        <m>b_{2m+1} \to 0</m>, <m>S_{2m+1} \to L</m> as well.
        Directly: <m>(S_{2m+1})</m> is decreasing and bounded below
        by 0, also by MCT.
        (c) Both subsequences have limit <m>L</m>; since every term
        of <m>(S_n)</m> is either an even or odd partial sum, and
        both subsequences converge to <m>L</m>, the whole sequence
        does. (Formally: for any <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, pick
        <m>N</m> so that both <m>|S_{2m} - L|</m> and <m>|S_{2m+1} - L|</m>
        are less than <m>\varepsilon</m> for <m>2m, 2m+1 \geq N</m>.)
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-sg-26-6">
    <title>Reading Ahead: The Integral Test</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Zorn &#xa7;2.6 (or &#xa7;2.5 problem set) mentions the
        Integral Test: if <m>f(x) \geq 0</m> is continuous and
        decreasing on <m>[1, \infty)</m>, then
        <m>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} f(k)</m> converges if and only if
        the improper integral <m>\int_1^{\infty} f(x)\,dx</m>
        converges.
      </p>
      <p>
        Without proving anything: use the Integral Test (informally)
        to argue that <m>\sum 1/k^p</m> converges for <m>p \gt 1</m>
        and diverges for <m>p \leq 1</m>. (You may appeal to
        calculus-level facts about <m>\int_1^{\infty} x^{-p}\,dx</m>.)
      </p>
      <p>
        Note: the Integral Test will not be used in the worked
        examples or on the module assessment. It is covered here
        because Bauldry states it in the Bridge Reading, and it
        foreshadows the precise integral/series comparisons developed
        in MAT 5610 via the Riemann integral.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\int_1^{\infty} x^{-p}\,dx</m> converges if <m>p \gt 1</m>
        (to <m>1/(p-1)</m>) and diverges if <m>p \leq 1</m>. So by the
        Integral Test, <m>\sum 1/k^p</m> converges exactly when
        <m>p \gt 1</m>. This recovers the <m>p</m>-series theorem.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

</section>
